The initial code contains several bugs and inefficiencies. First, the .sort() method without a comparator function sorts elements as strings and not numbers which results in incorrect sorting for numeric arrays. Secondly, the function returns arr[1] assuming the array sorted in ascending order but it actually returns the second smallest element not the second largest. Additionally sorting the entire array has a time complexity of O(n log n) which is not optimal for just finding the second largest number.

Below is the corrected function that uses a single pass approach to find the second largest number with a time complexity of O(n). The logic keeps track of the largest and second largest numbers during one traversal of the array. This avoids the overhead of sorting while correctly handling arrays of arbitrary integers.

```javascript
function secondLargest(arr) {
    // Edge case: If array length is less than 2 return null since no second largest exists
    if (arr.length < 2) return null;

    // Initialize two variables largest and secondLargest
    // Set them to the smallest possible number for comparison
    let largest = -Infinity;
    let secondLargest = -Infinity;

    // Iterate over each value in arr
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        let num = arr[i];

        // If current number is greater than largest update largest and secondLargest
        if (num > largest) {
            secondLargest = largest;
            largest = num;
        } 
        // If current number is different than largest but greater than secondLargest update secondLargest
        else if (num > secondLargest && num !== largest) {
            secondLargest = num;
        }
    }

    // If secondLargest was not updated return null otherwise return the value
    return secondLargest === -Infinity ? null : secondLargest;
}
```

This function efficiently finds the second largest value by scanning the array once. It correctly handles cases such as all elements being equal or array sizes less than two. Using -Infinity ensures no number in the input array will fail the initial comparisons. This optimized approach avoids sorting overhead and gives the correct second largest number for any integer array input.

